package com.bqsqwad.heartbreakhealer.feature.entertainment

/** Original ten-part story: The Lanterns of Market Street. */
internal object StoryLibraryBatch55 {
    val stories = listOf(
        StorySeries(
            id = "the_lanterns_of_market_street",
            title = "The Lanterns of Market Street",
            tagline = "A group of students researches the history of a street festival and helps plan a safer, more inclusive return.",
            parts = listOf(
                StoryPart(1, "A Street Without Lanterns", listOf(
                    "Market Street used to glow with paper lanterns during the town's annual evening festival. The tradition had paused several years earlier, and the decorations were stored away. When the student council proposed bringing the festival back, Ayo asked why it had stopped. The teacher adviser suggested beginning with the event records rather than relying on rumours.",
                    "The school archive held photographs of lanterns above the street, but no recent safety plan. A note said the event had been paused while the council reviewed crowd movement and electrical arrangements. Ayo and her friend Ife agreed to research the history and ask the organisers what would be needed for a responsible return."
                )),
                StoryPart(2, "The Festival Committee", listOf(
                    "The old committee's public minutes showed that local traders, residents, and school groups had helped organise the event. Lanterns were made in workshops and displayed along approved routes. The committee had always coordinated with the council, market managers, and emergency services. The students contacted the current community events office for guidance.",
                    "The events officer said a new festival would need a fresh application, risk assessment, and route approval. The old plan could be used as historical context but not copied as a current operating plan. The students accepted that their role was to help with research and creative ideas, while trained organisers handled public safety."
                )),
                StoryPart(3, "The Lantern Makers", listOf(
                    "A retired craft teacher, Mrs Ekanem, had led one of the original workshops. She showed the students examples of lanterns made from paper and lightweight frames. She explained that each group had followed a design guide so decorations would be suitable for the approved display areas. The students asked whether she would teach a small workshop at school.",
                    "Mrs Ekanem agreed, provided the school checked materials and supervised the activity. She also asked the students to avoid open flames and use safe battery-powered lights only if the event organisers approved them. Ayo added the safety note to the workshop plan. The aim was to celebrate the tradition without recreating avoidable risks."
                )),
                StoryPart(4, "A Route for Everyone", listOf(
                    "The council's draft event guidance identified the market entrance, bus stop, emergency access lane, and accessible paths. The students learned that a festival route had to work for traders, visitors, people with mobility needs, and emergency teams. Ife suggested making a map that clearly showed entrances, exits, quiet areas, and information points.",
                    "The events officer reviewed their draft and corrected two locations. The students added a note that the final route would be published only after formal approval. They did not advertise a date or invite crowds before the council had confirmed the arrangements. The planning work was useful even while the event remained uncertain."
                )),
                StoryPart(5, "A Workshop for All", listOf(
                    "The school hosted a lantern workshop with several design choices: folding, drawing, pattern-making, and writing short messages. Students could participate without displaying their work publicly. Mrs Ekanem showed how traditional patterns had varied between groups and encouraged everyone to credit the makers whose designs they used.",
                    "The workshop produced a set of lightweight lantern designs for review. The teacher checked the materials and stored them safely. Ayo asked participants whether they wanted their names attached to their work; some agreed, while others preferred group credit. The club recorded those choices so the display would respect each maker."
                )),
                StoryPart(6, "The Quiet Corner", listOf(
                    "The events office asked for ideas to make the festival comfortable for visitors who might prefer a calmer space. The student council suggested a quiet corner away from the loudest activities, with seating and clear signs. The market manager said the space had to remain outside the delivery route and be supervised by event staff.",
                    "Ife helped create a simple sign using large lettering and clear symbols. The events officer reviewed the location and said the final placement would depend on the approved site plan. The students understood that inclusion was not an extra decoration; it had to be built into the event's practical arrangements."
                )),
                StoryPart(7, "The Decision", listOf(
                    "After reviewing the application, the council approved a smaller festival on a limited route, with specific timings and trained stewards. The organisers published the approved information and explained that some parts of the old route would not be used. The student council shared the notice only after checking it against the official announcement.",
                    "Ayo was pleased but knew the students had not secured the approval by themselves. The event office, market managers, maintenance staff, and safety teams had completed the necessary work. The school would contribute the lantern display and a short history panel, following the agreed instructions."
                )),
                StoryPart(8, "The Evening of Light", listOf(
                    "On festival evening, stewards guided visitors along the approved route. The lanterns were displayed above the pedestrian area, clear of signs and access points. Battery-powered lights were used according to the event plan. The quiet corner remained available, and the information desk helped visitors find the facilities.",
                    "Mrs Ekanem watched as families stopped to look at the student designs. She recognised a pattern from the earliest festival and explained its history to a small group. Ayo and Ife helped at the school display, answering questions without blocking the walkway. The event felt lively because its planning had left room for different needs."
                )),
                StoryPart(9, "A Record of the Return", listOf(
                    "After the festival, the organisers held a review meeting. They discussed visitor feedback, steward reports, waste collection, and suggestions for next year. The students submitted their history panel and workshop notes. The events officer thanked them but reminded the group that any future event would need a new review and approval.",
                    "The school archive added photographs of the approved display and a copy of the public event notice. The students included the date and explained that the festival had returned in a smaller format after a formal safety review. They did not present one successful evening as a guarantee that every future plan would be identical."
                )),
                StoryPart(10, "Lanterns for Tomorrow", listOf(
                    "The town decided to consider another festival the following year, using the review findings and a new application. Mrs Ekanem donated a sample lantern to the school archive, while the students kept their workshop guide for future classes. The tradition had returned, but it had also learned to adapt.",
                    "Ayo looked at the lanterns as they were carefully packed away. Their glow had been temporary, but the teamwork behind them would last longer. The students had helped preserve a tradition by respecting its history, following expert guidance, and making space for more people to enjoy it. Market Street had found its light again, one thoughtful decision at a time."
                ))
            )
        )
    )
}
